.TH SSHI 1 "2026-11-23" "SSHI 0.2.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sshi, sshi-copy-id, sshi-copy-iid \- use OpenSSH over an iroh relay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshi
.RB [ \-i " file" ]
.RB [ \-L " forward" ]
.RB [ \-R " forward" ]
.RB [ \-D " address" ]
.RB [ \-M ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-S " control_path" ]
.RB [ \-O " check|exit" ]
.RB [ \-o " ControlPersist=time" ]
.RB [ \-N ]
.RB [ \-T | \-t ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.I user@endpoint_id
.RI [ command " [argument ...]]"
.br
.B sshi id
.br
.B sshi list
.br
.B sshi-copy-iid
.RB [ \-p " port" ]
.RB [ \-i " ssh_key" ]
.RB [ \-\-client\-id " endpoint_id" ]
.I user@host_or_endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi-copy-iid \-\-invite
.I user@endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi-copy-id
.RB [ \-i " public_key" ]
.RB [ \-\-login\-key " private_key" ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.I user@endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi allow-client
.I endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi invite
.I endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi revoke-invite
.I endpoint_id
.br
.B sshi serve
.RB [ \-\-allow\-client " endpoint_id" ]
.RB [ \-\-target " address:port" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshi
connects the local OpenSSH client to a receiving computer identified by its
iroh Endpoint ID. The tunnel uses the configured iroh relay for all traffic
between the two computers. OpenSSH performs login authentication and host key
verification. The receiving computer runs an OpenSSH server on its loopback
interface. The SSH account is the part of the destination before the at sign.
If the receiver declines the initiating Endpoint ID during an interactive login,
sshi shows the client's public ID and prompts for an invitation code or link.
After the invitation is accepted, SSH login continues. Press Enter at the empty
prompt to cancel. For scripts, use the invitation redeem command explicitly.
An OpenSSH ConnectTimeout also limits the time available to enter a code
during login. Use the separate invitation redeem command when awaiting a code.
Multiple SSH sessions to the same receiver use a common iroh tunnel with
receivers that support it. The tunnel closes after the last session ends.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B id
Print the local user's public iroh Endpoint ID. Create the persistent identity
if necessary. This command works offline.
.TP
.B list
List the local user's successfully authenticated SSH destinations, with the
most recently used first. Repeated destinations appear once.
.TP
.B sshi-desktop
Open the GTK connection manager on Linux. On macOS open the SSHI application.
Both use the same saved profiles, relay settings and history as the CLI.
.TP
.B profile list [\-\-json]
Show saved profiles.
.TP
.B profile save \-\-name NAME \-\-user USER \-\-endpoint ID [\-\-relay URL] [\-\-previous NAME]
Create a named connection or rename/edit the specified previous entry.
Omit the relay to use this account's default.
.TP
.B profile rename \-\-name NAME \-\-to NEW_NAME
Rename one saved connection. The change preserves its user, endpoint and relay.
.TP
.B profile remove \-\-name NAME
Remove the named connection.
.TP
.B profile run \-\-name NAME \-\- ARGS
Run another sshi command with the selected profile's relay settings.
.TP
.B connect NAME [COMMAND ...]
Start an SSH session or run a command using a saved profile.
.TP
.B forward \-\-profile NAME \-\-kind local|remote|dynamic \-\-bind\-port PORT
Start a dedicated OpenSSH forwarding session using a saved profile.
The listen address defaults to 127.0.0.1 and can be changed with
.BR \-\-bind\-host .
For local and remote forwarding, supply both
.B \-\-target\-host
and
.BR \-\-target\-port .
A dynamic forward is a local SOCKS proxy. Applications choose their own destinations.
Ports range from 1 through 65535. IPv6 addresses may be entered with or without brackets.
Press Ctrl+C in the forwarding terminal to stop.
.TP
.B socket start [\-\-persist TIME] PROFILE
Authenticate through the selected Iroh relay, then create a local OpenSSH control
socket in a private directory under /tmp. Starting the same active master again
returns its socket path. The default idle timeout is 700 minutes.
.TP
.B socket path|command PROFILE
Print the local socket path or a quoted shell command for connecting through it.
Append a remote command or use it as printed for an interactive shell.
Both operations work before a socket has been opened.
.TP
.B socket check|close PROFILE
Check whether the local SSH master is running, or stop it and its attached sessions.
.TP
.B update [\-\-json]
Check the latest stable release on GitHub and show the package for the current
platform, or the release page when no matching package is available. The graphical
client checks in the background and offers a download when a newer version exists.
.TP
.B backup FILE
Write all named connections and connection history to a new portable JSON file.
Credentials, SSH keys, server fingerprints and the private Iroh identity remain
in their local stores. The output file must have a new name.
.TP
.B restore FILE
Merge a supported JSON backup. Conflicting names abort the import
before any connection is changed. Matching entries are preserved.
.TP
.B config show [\-\-json]
Show effective settings and their sources. Token values stay private.
.TP
.B config set-relay URL
Save this user's preferred relay address.
.TP
.B config reset-relay
Remove the user's relay override and return to inherited settings.
.TP
.B config set-token [\-\-relay URL]
Read the token from a hidden prompt or standard input. Store it in the
current user's private configuration directory for this relay.
.TP
.B issue-invite \-\-name NAME \-\-client ID [\-\-service-user USER] [\-\-share]
Use an existing authorized connection to issue an invitation on its receiver.
Specify the receiver service account when it differs from the SSH account.
The remote Endpoint ID is checked before issuing a code.
With
.B \-\-share
print a full invitation link including the profile's SSH user and relay.
.TP
.B invite CLIENT_ID \-\-share \-\-user SSH_USER [\-\-relay URL]
Issue a targeted invitation on this receiver and print a single SSHI link.
Share it with the owner of the invited client. The link includes the receiver
Endpoint ID, SSH username, relay URL and invitation code.
.TP
.B invitation inspect
Read a shared link from standard input and print its public connection fields
as JSON. The invitation code is omitted.
.TP
.B invitation redeem [\-\-endpoint RECEIVER_ID] [\-\-relay URL]
Enter the link at the hidden prompt or pipe it to standard input. For a
standalone invitation code, specify the receiver Endpoint ID. The receiver
verifies the client identity and the code. After acceptance, connect with
the receiver's SSH username and your normal SSH key or password.
.TP
.B copy-iid
Authorize a public iroh Endpoint ID on a receiver.
Use the receiver's regular SSH address or connect through iroh by specifying
its Endpoint ID. An iroh connection requires an already authorized client
identity for SSH access. Use an existing SSH account with permission to edit the receiver
configuration. The receiver must have the
.B sshi allow-client
command installed. Also available as
.BR sshi-copy-iid .
The command adds the local Endpoint ID by default. Set
.B \-\-client\-id
to add the public Endpoint ID of another computer.
The optional
.B \-p
selects the port for direct SSH and
.B \-i
selects an existing private SSH key for authentication.
.B \-\-invite
instead redeems an invitation through the receiver's iroh Endpoint ID.
The invitation code is read from a hidden terminal prompt or standard input.
.TP
.B copy-id
Install a public OpenSSH key in the remote account's
.IR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
over iroh. Also available as
.BR sshi-copy-id .
Use
.B \-i
with a public key file or the base name of its
.B .pub
companion. Without
.BR \-i ,
the command looks for a standard public key in
.IR ~/.ssh .
Use
.B \-\-login\-key
to authenticate with an existing private key, or
.B \-n
to validate the public key and show the destination.
The remote host uses
.BR ssh-keygen (1)
and
.BR flock (1)
when available. Receivers running SSHI 0.2.0 can also lock the key file directly on macOS.
.TP
.BI "allow-client " endpoint_id
Add the given public Endpoint ID to the local
.B allow_clients
array. Applies to new connections immediately with an updated
.B sshi serve
process. Repeating the command leaves the entry in place.
.TP
.BI "invite " endpoint_id
Issue a code for the client Endpoint ID and print it to standard output.
Run this command as the user running
.B sshi serve
on the receiver. The code expires after ten minutes. Successful redemption
adds access to the receiver's saved state. Subsequent SSH connections still
use the server's SSH authentication.
.TP
.BI "revoke-invite " endpoint_id
Revoke a pending invitation or the access granted by it. Run this command
as the same receiver user. The change applies to new connections.
.TP
.B serve
Register the same identity printed by
.B sshi id
and forward authorized incoming connections to the local OpenSSH server.
.B \-\-allow\-client
adds an incoming Endpoint ID to the configured allowlist and may be repeated.
The TOML allowlist is read for each new connection.
An empty allowlist denies incoming connections. The local user's outgoing
connections also use the service while it is running.
.B \-\-target
overrides the local OpenSSH server address for this run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " file"
Use the specified SSH private key. May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-L " [bind_address:]port:host:hostport"
Listen on the initiating computer and connect to the destination from the
receiving computer. OpenSSH Unix socket syntax is also supported.
.TP
.BI \-R " [bind_address:]port:host:hostport"
Listen on the receiving computer and connect to the destination from the
initiating computer. OpenSSH Unix socket syntax is also supported.
.TP
.BI \-D " [bind_address:]port"
Provide a local SOCKS proxy through the receiving computer.
.TP
.B \-N
Run without a remote command or shell. Use with port forwarding and press
Ctrl+C to stop.
.TP
.B \-T
Disable remote pseudo-terminal allocation.
.TP
.B \-t
Request a remote pseudo-terminal. Use twice to request it with redirected stdin.
.TP
.B \-v
Enable OpenSSH diagnostics. Repeat for increased verbosity.
.PP
The forwarding options may be repeated. The SSH server's forwarding policy
applies. When a requested listener cannot be set up, the SSH client exits with
an error.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B /etc/sshi/config.toml
System-wide TOML configuration. Set
.B relay_url
to the same relay URL on both computers. Set
.B allow_clients
to an array of authorized initiating Endpoint IDs on the receiving computer.
.B ssh_target
defaults to
.BR 127.0.0.1:22 .
.B relay_token_file
can specify the absolute path to a relay token file readable by the user.
The optional per-user token file defaults to
.BR ~/.config/sshi/relay.token .
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/sshi/
Per-user identity, connection library, history and invitation grants. The default directory is the same on Linux and macOS.
.PP
.B SSHI_CONFIG
overrides the system-wide configuration path.
.B IROH_RELAY_URL
and
.B IROH_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN
override the configured relay URL and token.
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
and
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
override the defaults for per-user configuration and state respectively.
.PP
After updating the package or changing a receiving service's relay, token or
SSH target, restart that service. Each incoming connection checks current
allowlist and invitation permissions. Before using another relay or token for
a shared client identity, close its existing sessions on the prior relay.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set PEER to the receiving computer's output from
.BR "sshi id" .
.PP
Log in, or run a single command:
.PP
.nf
sshi root@"$PEER"
sshi root@"$PEER" uname \-a
.fi
.PP
Authorize this computer on a receiver using its ordinary SSH address:
.PP
.nf
sshi-copy-iid root@raspberrypi.local
.fi
.PP
Add a new client's public ID over iroh from an already authorized computer:
.PP
.nf
sshi-copy-iid \-\-client\-id "$NEW_CLIENT_ID" root@"$PEER"
.fi
.PP
Issue an invitation on the receiver and redeem it on the new client:
.PP
.nf
sshi invite "$NEW_CLIENT_ID"
sshi-copy-iid \-\-invite root@"$PEER"
.fi
.PP
Install a public SSH key for future logins:
.PP
.nf
sshi-copy-id \-i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub root@"$PEER"
.fi
.PP
Forward local TCP port 8080 to port 80 on the receiving computer:
.PP
.nf
sshi \-N \-L 127.0.0.1:8080:127.0.0.1:80 root@"$PEER"
.fi
.PP
Forward port 9000 on the receiving computer to local port 3000:
.PP
.nf
sshi \-N \-R 127.0.0.1:9000:127.0.0.1:3000 root@"$PEER"
.fi
.PP
Create a local SOCKS listener on port 1080:
.PP
.nf
sshi \-N \-D 127.0.0.1:1080 root@"$PEER"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR sshd_config (5)
